41389,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.00119957}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_id":"ENSG00000108557","module":"green","kme":0.118896,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.416898,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000108557","module":"yellow","kme":0.164861,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.41964,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000108557","module":"royalblue","kme":0.0830095,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.683527,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000108557","entrez_id":["10743"],"uniprot":["Q7Z5J4"],"ncbi_summary":"This gene is located within the Smith-Magenis syndrome region on chromosome 17. It is highly similar to its mouse counterpart and is expressed at high levels mainly in neuronal tissues. The protein encoded by this gene includes a polymorphic polyglutamine tract in the N-terminal domain. Expression of the mouse counterpart in neurons is induced by retinoic acid. This gene is associated with both the severity of the phenotype and the response to medication in schizophrenic patients. [provided by RefSeq, Jul 2008].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q7Z5J4","text":"Transcriptional regulator of the circadian clock components: CLOCK, BMAL1, BMAL2, PER1/3, CRY1/2, NR1D1/2 and RORA/C. Positively regulates the transcriptional activity of CLOCK a core component of the circadian clock. Regulates transcription through chromatin remodeling by interacting with other proteins in chromatin as well as proteins in the basic transcriptional machinery. May be important for embryonic and postnatal development. May be involved in neuronal differentiation."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}